.\" Install:      cp man/man1/scalaall.1 /usr/local/share/man/man1/
.TH SCALAALL 1 "2026" "scalars 0.1.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
scalaall \- the scalars comprehensive reference (invocation, language
slice, bytecode VM, Cranelift JIT, numeric semantics, editor/debugger
tooling)
.SH OVERVIEW
This is the all\-in\-one
.B scalars
manual, modeled on
.BR zshall (1).
It concatenates the major sections of the scalars reference into a single
page so you can grep with `/`:
.RS 2
.PP
.B 1.  INVOCATION
.RB "\(em " "file run + introspection + LSP/DAP flags"
.br
.B 2.  LANGUAGE SLICE
.RB "\(em " "entry object, statements, expressions, operators"
.br
.B 3.  VALUE MODEL
.RB "\(em " "Int/Double/Boolean/String/null on the fusevm value model"
.br
.B 4.  NUMERIC SEMANTICS
.RB "\(em " "Int/Double division dispatch, String +, structural ==, Double.toString"
.br
.B 5.  BYTECODE VM
.RB "\(em " "lowering to fusevm ops, no bespoke VM, no JVM"
.br
.B 6.  CRANELIFT JIT
.RB "\(em " "tiered compilation of hot loops"
.br
.B 7.  LSP SERVER
.RB "\(em " "completion, hover, live diagnostics"
.br
.B 8.  DAP ADAPTER
.RB "\(em " "source\-line breakpoints, stepping, variables"
.br
.B 9.  ENVIRONMENT
.br
.B 10. EXIT STATUS
.br
.B 11. FILES
.br
.B 12. EXAMPLES
.br
.B 13. GAPS
.RE
.PP
For a shorter quick\-reference page see
.BR scala (1).
.SH "1.  INVOCATION"
The program text is a single
.B .scala
file named as the first non\-option argument.  Arguments after the file
are collected but not yet exposed to the program (the
.B args
parameter of
.B main
is parsed and ignored).
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-tokens
Print the lexer token stream and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-ast
Print the parsed AST and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-disasm
Print the lowered fusevm bytecode disassembly and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-lsp
Speak the Language Server Protocol over stdio.
.TP
.B \-\-dap
Speak the Debug Adapter Protocol over stdio.
.TP
.BR \-version ", " \-\-version
Print the version banner and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the option grammar and exit.
.SH "2.  LANGUAGE SLICE"
.SS Entry object
A compilation unit declares an entry
.BR object ,
optionally alongside top\-level
.BR class ,
.BR "case class" ,
.B object
and
.B trait
declarations.  scalars finds the entry point two ways:
.B object Name extends App { <body> }
runs
.B <body>
directly, and
.B object Name { ... def main(args: Array[String]): Unit = { <body> } ... }
runs its
.BR main .
Optional
.BR package / import
prologue lines are tolerated and ignored.
.SS Statements
.BR val / var
bindings (with an optional
.BR ": Type"
annotation), assignment and compound assignment
.RB ( = ,
.BR += ,
.BR -= ,
.BR *= ,
.BR /= ,
.BR %= ,
and the growable\-collection
.BR ++= / --= ),
.B if (cond) { .. } else { .. }
,
.B while (cond) { .. }
,
.B for
in every form (range and collection generators, several generators,
.B if
guards, destructuring binders, and
.BR yield ),
.BR def
declarations \(em including block\-local ones \(em
.BR return ,
.BR try / catch / finally ,
.BR throw ,
and
.BR println / print .
Statements are separated by inferred line breaks or explicit
.BR ; .
.SS Expressions
Integer, floating, string (including
.BR s / f
interpolation), char (as a one\-char string), boolean, and
.B null
literals; variable reads; unary
.BR - / ! ;
.B if
and block expressions;
.BR match
with literal, binder, typed, tuple, constructor and stable\-identifier
patterns;
.B new
construction and method / field access; function literals
.RB ( "x => e" ,
.BR "{ case ... }" ,
.B _
placeholders, in the parenthesized and the brace form of an argument alike \-
.BR "xs.map { _ * 2 }" ,
.BR "xs.foldLeft(0) { _ + _ }" );
tuples; and the collection literals.
.SS Operators
.B + - * / %
(arithmetic),
.B == != < > <= >=
(comparison),
.B && ||
(short\-circuiting boolean),
.B :: ++ :+ +: ->
(collection), and general infix method syntax \(em
.B a m b
is
.B a.m(b)
for any single\-argument method.
.SS Newline inference
The hand\-written lexer emits a statement\-separator line break only where
the previous token can end a statement, the next can begin one, and the
cursor is not inside
.BR (...) / [...] .
.SH "3.  VALUE MODEL"
Scalars maps the primitives straight onto the fusevm value model:
.BR Int
as a native integer,
.BR Double
as a float,
.BR Boolean
as a bool,
.BR String
as a VM string, and the
.B null
literal as
.BR Value::Undef .
Everything with structure \(em class and
.B case class
instances, collections, tuples, function values and throwables \(em lives
in a frontend\-owned object heap behind fusevm's opaque
.B Value::Obj
handle, so no fusevm change was needed to carry them.  Top\-level bindings
are addressed by name through
.BR GetVar / SetVar ;
a
.B def
body and a function literal each get a native
.B Op::Call
frame whose parameters, captured upvalues and locals are frame slots.
.SH "4.  NUMERIC SEMANTICS"
.SS Division
fusevm's native division always floats, but Scala's
.B /
truncates for two
.BR Int s
.RB ( "7 / 2 == 3" )
and floats when either operand is a
.B Double
.RB ( "7 / 2.0 == 3.5" ).
Because scalars carries no static types, the choice is made at runtime by
a type\-dispatching
.B SDIV
builtin.  Integer division by zero yields
.B null
(a documented gap; Scala throws
.BR ArithmeticException ).
.SS String +
Scala's
.B +
is string concatenation when a
.B String
operand is involved:
.B String.+(Any)
(a String left operand) and numeric
.B +(String)
(a numeric left operand, String right).  Other mixed
.B +
operands are rejected rather than silently concatenated.
.SS Structural ==
.B ==
/
.B !=
compare by value (Scala's
.B ==
is
.BR equals ),
so strings compare by content.
.SS Double.toString
Whole doubles print with a trailing
.B .0
; magnitude selects decimal notation for
.B 1e-3 <= |x| < 1e7
and computerized scientific
.RB ( 1.0E7 ,
.BR 1.0E-4 )
otherwise, reproducing Java's
.BR Double.toString .
Non\-finite values print
.BR NaN / Infinity / -Infinity .
.SH "5.  BYTECODE VM"
.B scalars
lowers the Scala AST to a flat
.B fusevm::Chunk
of stack\-VM ops
.RB ( LoadInt ,
.BR Add ,
.BR GetVar ,
.BR JumpIfFalse ,
.BR CallBuiltin ,
\(em) and runs it on the shared engine.  There is no local
.BR vm.rs / jit.rs
and no JVM.  Short\-circuit
.BR && / || ,
control flow, and the range
.B for
resolve to jump\-patched offsets at compile time; a
.B for
snapshots its upper bound into a synthetic local, matching Scala's
evaluate\-the\-range\-once semantics.
.SH "6.  CRANELIFT JIT"
Execution runs on fusevm's three\-tier Cranelift JIT; hot loops
trace\-compile to native code, and the
.B jit-disk-cache
feature persists compiled native code across runs keyed by chunk hash.
Most arithmetic stays on the native fast path. Operations with a
non\-numeric operand delegate to the strict numeric hook in the host, as
do the two numeric cases an f64 cannot answer exactly: integer overflow
(Scala's Long wraps) and a mixed Int/Double pair whose integer is past
2^53, where the hook applies Scala's widening to Double.
.SH "7.  LSP SERVER"
.B scala --lsp
speaks the Language Server Protocol over stdio.  It is self\-contained and
read\-only:
.IP \[bu] 2
.B completion
of the full reference corpus \(em keywords, declarations, patterns,
collection / String / numeric methods, scala.math, throwables, operators
and Predef;
.IP \[bu] 2
.B hover
rendering each name's chapter, one\-line doc, signature, and a runnable
example;
.IP \[bu] 2
.B diagnostics
computed by the runtime's own parser \(em a syntax error maps to a
diagnostic on the reported line.
.PP
The same corpus generates
.B docs/reference.html
via the
.B gen-docs
binary, so the editor and the static reference never drift.
.SH "8.  DAP ADAPTER"
.B scala --dap
speaks the Debug Adapter Protocol over stdio: a single\-threaded
source\-line debugger.  The program is compiled with a per\-statement
.B DBG_LINE
marker (emitted only in this mode) and run on the pure interpreter, so
breakpoints and stepping land on real lines.  Supported requests:
.BR setBreakpoints ,
.BR stackTrace ,
.BR scopes ,
.BR variables ,
.BR evaluate
(bare local lookup),
.BR continue ,
.BR next ,
.BR stepIn ,
.BR stepOut .
Program stdout is redirected to a pipe during the run and forwarded as
.B output
events, so
.BR println / print
never corrupt the JSON protocol channel.
.SH "9.  ENVIRONMENT"
scalars reads no environment variables of its own.
The shared fusevm JIT / disk\-cache honor their own variables.
.SH "10. EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
Error during lexing, parsing, lowering, or execution.  Errors are written
to stderr as
.BR "scalars: <reason>" .
.SH "11. FILES"
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/man/man1/scala.1
Short reference man page.
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/man/man1/scalaall.1
This man page.
.SH "12. EXAMPLES"
.PP
Run a Scala file:
.RS
.nf
scala hello.scala
.fi
.RE
.PP
Print the token stream, the AST, or the bytecode:
.RS
.nf
scala --dump-tokens hello.scala
scala --dump-ast   hello.scala
scala --disasm     hello.scala
.fi
.RE
.PP
An entry object with a range loop and Int/Double division:
.RS
.nf
object Demo extends App {
  var sum = 0
  for (i <- 1 to 10) { sum += i }
  println(sum)              // 55
  println(7 / 2)            // 3
  println(7 / 2.0)          // 3.5
  println("total = " + sum) // total = 55
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Editor / debugger tooling over stdio:
.RS
.nf
scala --lsp
scala --dap
.fi
.RE
.SH "13. GAPS"
Documented gaps (see
.B BUGS.md
in the source tree for the full ledger):
.IP \[bu] 2
Lazy views and iterators \(em
.BR .view ,
.BR .iterator ,
and
.BR LazyList .
.BR grouped / sliding
answer the
.B List
of windows rather than an
.BR Iterator .
.IP \[bu] 2
Sorting by a user
.BR Ordering ,
and
.BR Ordered / Comparable
on a user class.  The built\-in ordering covers numbers,
.BR String ,
.BR Boolean
and tuples of those.
.IP \[bu] 2
The insertion\-ordered
.BR LinkedHashSet / LinkedHashMap ,
and the rest of
.BR scala.collection.mutable
.RB ( Queue ,
.BR Stack ,
.BR ArrayDeque ).
They are an unresolved name rather than an alias onto the hash\-table
forms, whose order differs.
.IP \[bu] 2
The symbolic operators
.BR /: ,
.BR :\e ,
and user\-defined symbolic method names.
.IP \[bu] 2
The wider standard library \(em
.BR scala.io ,
.B scala.collection
as a namespace, and the
.BR String /numeric
methods beyond the wired subset.
.IP \[bu] 2
Types are parsed but not checked, and
.B Int
arithmetic uses 64\-bit semantics rather than wrapping at 32 bits.
